<title>INDIA: Lower supplies drive Indian soybean prices higher.</title>
<headline>Lower supplies drive Indian soybean prices higher.</headline>
<dateline>INDORE, India 1997-01-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Soybean prices rose 100 rupees a tonne on Friday with arrivals down at 2,500 tonnes against 20,000 tonnes a day year-ago, traders said.</p>
<p>&quot;Prices may further go up due to very limited availability against good demand from processing plants,&quot; one dealer said.</p>
<p>Soyoil refined rose by 100 rupees per tonne on good demand in local market. Soyoil solvent was steady in light volume.</p>
<p>Soymeal and rapeseed extraction prices were steady in limited trading activity. Soymeal yellow was quoted at $270-272 per tonne and rapeseed extraction at $126-127 per tonne for exports. Rapeseed extraction was 4,325 rupees FOR Bedibunder and was 4,300 rupees FOR Bhavnagar. Business activity was at the lowest level.</p>
